{"name":"Colonnade Stone","hex":"#c6c0b6","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"colonnade","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A series of columns at regular intervals.","A peristyle."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/colonnade"},{"term":"stone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard earthen substance that can form rocks; especially, such substance when regarded as a building material.","A piece of such material: a block of stone, rock or a pebble."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Constructed of stone.","Having the appearance of stone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Colonnade","blocks":[{"text":"In classical architecture, a colonnade is a long sequence of columns joined by their entablature, often free-standing, or part of a building. Paired or multiple pairs of columns are normally employed in a colonnade which can be straight or curved. The space enclosed may be covered or open. In St. Peter's Square in Rome, Bernini's great colonnade encloses a vast open elliptical space."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Wikimedia_Conference_2015_photo_by_Pine_-_28.jpg/330px-Wikimedia_Conference_2015_photo_by_Pine_-_28.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colonnade","wikidata":"Q657100"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Wikimedia_Conference_2015_photo_by_Pine_-_28.jpg/500px-Wikimedia_Conference_2015_photo_by_Pine_-_28.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Wikimedia_Conference_2015_photo_by_Pine_-_28.jpg/1280px-Wikimedia_Conference_2015_photo_by_Pine_-_28.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Wikimedia_Conference_2015